        {
          "path": "_m/d/j/e/4/united-states-army-aviation-museum-wp/rq-11a-raven-3896a-2151970963.jpg",
          "filename": "RQ-11A Raven 3896a (2151970963).jpg",
          "caption": "The Raven UAV \"has about 50 minutes of flight time on a battery. The kit comes with spare batteries and a charger so [the crew] can land it, pop in a spare and get it back in the air. It can go as fast as 90 [k/h], but usually cruises between 40 and 50. It can go as far as 15 kilometers from its controller on the ground, and usually flies a pre-programmed route, using GPS for navigation.\nThree different cameras can attach to the plane [electrical optical, infrared (nose), and a side-mounted IR]. The camera does not have zoom capability and is unable to lock on a target, but provides enough resolution to show someone carrying a weapon.\nRavens are easily hand-launched by turning on the motor and throwing it in the air. It lands by coming back to ground at a point designated by GPS location. . . On average, a Raven aircraft can survive about 200 landings before it breaks something.\"\n\nfrom Army Aviation Museum placard",
          "alt": "The Raven UAV \"has about 50 minutes of flight time on a battery. The kit comes with spare batteries and a charger so [the crew] can land it, pop in a spare and get it back in the air. It can go as fast as 90 [k/h], but usually cruises between 40 and 50. It can go as far as 15 kilometers from its controller on the ground, and usually flies a pre-programmed route, using GPS for navigation.\nThree different cameras can attach to the plane [electrical optical, infrared (nose), and a side-mounted IR]. The camera does not have zoom capability and is unable to lock on a target, but provides enough resolution to show someone carrying a weapon.\nRavens are easily hand-launched by turning on the motor and throwing it in the air. It lands by coming back to ground at a point designated by GPS location. . . On average, a Raven aircraft can survive about 200 landings before it breaks something.\"\n\nfrom Army Aviation Museum placard",
          "width": 2448,
          "height": 3264,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "James Emery from Douglasville, United States",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RQ-11A_Raven_3896a_(2151970963).jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "united-states-army-aviation-museum-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "dje4",
          "accessedAt": "2026-02-22T05:08:24.023Z",
          "mediumPath": "_m/d/j/e/4/united-states-army-aviation-museum-wp/rq-11a-raven-3896a-2151970963-medium.jpg",
          "smallPath": "_m/d/j/e/4/united-states-army-aviation-museum-wp/rq-11a-raven-3896a-2151970963-small.jpg"
